 "notes": [
  "Launch costs are a few microseconds against dense times near a millisecond, so H1 expects dense attention at N = 1024 to be limited by compute and memory traffic, not fixed costs.",
  "V2 cannot avoid four N x N passes through DRAM (about 1 ms here); V3 removes them, so fusion is predicted to matter about as much as tiling.",
  "G1 work is quantised into waves of thread blocks: at small M one partly filled wave sets the time (split-KV would lower it).",
  "Attention is only about a fifth of an encoder layer's FLOPs at this shape, so gating attention alone bounds the encoder-level saving; the token-wise layers need gating too.",
  "Break-even keep-rates are against the dense kernel's own time, not against running dense at a lower frame rate."
 ]
